WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.510
Maddy: There are certain barriers that an
organization is likely to run into when

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:09.120
they’re trying to change and innovate and,
in this video, we are going to look at

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.430
these and how to go about overcoming them.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:14.330
Phillip: So, what could these barriers be?

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:16.120
• Maddy: Fear of the unknown

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.360
• Phillip: Unclear goals

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.419
• Maddy: Lack of leadership

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.030
• Phillip: Unwillingness to change

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:20.950
• Maddy: Lack of planning

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.870
Phillip: There are some practical ways these
barriers can be overcome and some of these

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.300
solutions can be applied to more than one barrier.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.230
So, let’s have a look at
these in a little more detail.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.409
Maddy: Fear of the unknown

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.700
Uncertainty and anxiety around change
are very common and should be factored

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.360
into any innovations you’re planning.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:45.570
Although part of the reality of change is that
people will initially feel some discomfort around

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:50.759
it, it’s the uncertainty of what change involves
that can be the real driver behind the fear.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.790
Everyone who is affected by the changes should
understand what those changes are, when they’ll

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.260
be applied, and what they’ll mean for them.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.920
You can do this with efficient
and clear communication.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.840
Phillip: Unclear goals

00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:10.269
Something that will always make change and
innovation more difficult, is when it isn’t

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:12.750
made clear why the changes are necessary.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.990
Without clearly defined goals,
people may feel like change is being

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.630
pursued just for the sake of it.

00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:24.480
It is important to make sure that the
goal of all the changes and innovation,

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.349
such as increased efficiency, embracing
a new technology, or increasing profits,

00:01:29.629 --> 00:01:31.859
is made clear to everyone involved.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.190
This will help them get behind the
change and put all their energy into it.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.910
Again, clear, and effective communication,
like regular meetings and question

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:43.840
and answer sessions can help do this.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.399
Maddy: Lack of leadership

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:50.640
If it isn’t clear who’s in charge of leading
people through the change, or even worse if

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.190
no one is actually in charge, there will be
confusion, mistrust, and possibly even chaos.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.899
The nature of the leadership needed will
depend on the nature of the change in question.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:06.060
You might need a whole dedicated leadership
team if it’s a big, sweeping change that

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:12.240
effects everyone, or, you might just need a
change champion in each team or department.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.610
What’s most important is that everyone knows who
is in charge of the change, so they can act as

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.090
a point of contact and a source of reassurance.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.700
Phillip: Unwillingness to change

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:29.240
This can be a difficult barrier to overcome as it
involves winning over peoples’ hearts and minds.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:33.970
In order to inspire willingness to change,
you need to have already overcome many

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.890
of the barriers previously identified.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.700
There should be no uncertainty about
what the change involves, with clearly

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.920
defined the goals, and a leadership
structure that is clear to everyone.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.539
You can also help overcome the unwillingness
some people may feel to change by giving them

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.469
an avenue to express their worries, such as
through private meetings with a change leader

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:58.139
or through a dedicated channel on the Intranet.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:04.230
This will mean that they will feel listened to,
and if appropriate, you may even consider giving

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.890
them some responsibilities in achieving the
innovation, helping them to feel like they’re

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.109
on the inside, not the outside, of the change.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.640
Maddy: Lack of planning

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.769
Without some kind of plan, a
change is often doomed to fail.

00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:21.100
People won’t know what
they’re doing, when, or why!

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.290
The solution to this is simple,
make sure there is a plan.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:32.039
This should be prepared in advance of the change
and clearly define what will happen at each step.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:37.990
Phillip: It will need to be flexible enough to
absorb shocks from unexpected occurrences, but

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.209
not so flexible that it has no shape or structure.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.650
It should be prepared with input from the
right stakeholders in the organization,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:51.410
but if the planning involves too many
people, this may make it too complicated.

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:57.350
Maddy: What’s most important is that the plan
accurately describes the innovation, includes a

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:03.060
communications strategy, and clearly identifies
who is responsible for what aspects of the change.

